# 11. MOTION DESIGN & PHYSICS QUALITY

Motion quality is essential.

Avoid generic preset animations where every object moves identically.

## 11.1 Animation principles

Use:

- Smooth interpolation.
- Controlled acceleration.
- Natural deceleration.
- Different movement curves for different objects.
- Slight rotational inertia.
- Independent orbital speeds.
- Subtle depth-based scaling.
- Continuous animation without visible resets.
- Coordinated transitions between stages.

## 11.2 Performance requirements

Aim for a stable 60 FPS on capable devices, but do not sacrifice functionality on lower-performance hardware.

Use GPU-friendly properties where possible:

- `transform`
- `opacity`
- Efficient shader updates
- Instanced rendering where suitable
- Reusable geometry and materials
- Controlled particle counts

Avoid:

- Animating expensive layout properties every frame.
- Unnecessary React re-renders.
- Creating new geometries continuously.
- Recreating materials during every animation frame.
- Excessive blur layers.
- Unbounded particle systems.
- Memory leaks.
- Multiple animation loops running simultaneously.

Use requestAnimationFrame or the existing rendering engine appropriately.

Ensure all animation loops, event listeners, and rendering resources are cleaned up when the component unmounts.

## 11.3 Adaptive rendering

The feature must remain usable on:

- Desktop.
- Laptop.
- Tablet.
- Mobile.

On lower-performance devices:

- Reduce particle density.
- Simplify secondary lighting.
- Reduce expensive blur.
- Use fewer decorative effects.
- Preserve the core interaction.
- Maintain all essential navigation controls.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

When reduced motion is enabled, replace long cinematic transitions with shorter, less intense transitions while preserving the complete interaction flow.

